# OCI CLI — {% if mode == 'create' %}create standby and associate{% else %}check existing database pair{% endif %}

Requires Bash, Python 3 and an installed, configured OCI CLI. Use your normal OCI
CLI authentication profile (`oci setup config`, or supported OCI CLI environment
variables). Run from your workstation or OCI Cloud Shell.

Review `request.json` and `run.sh`, then run:

```sh
bash run.sh
```

The script locates request.json relative to itself, so it also runs from another directory.
{% if mode == 'create' %}

**Running this script creates a new standby database.** Apply and verify networking
first. Ensure the primary and target support this configuration, have compatible
database versions, sufficient capacity, IAM permissions and licensing entitlement.
{% if creation_type == 'ExistingDbSystem' %}
The existing DB system target is for bare metal / Exadata, not Base Database VM systems.
{% endif %}
Active Data Guard defaults to enabled. MAXIMUM_PERFORMANCE and ASYNC are used.
The same admin password as the primary is requested securely at runtime and sent
to the CLI through standard input. It is not in the generated JSON, shell history,
process arguments or a temporary file. Run from an interactive terminal; do not
use OCI debug logging with sensitive requests.

A successful command returns the association request details; standby creation
continues in OCI. Verify completion and replication health in the OCI console.
This script has no Terraform state or prevent_destroy protection. Do not run it
and the Terraform creation configuration for the same standby; choose one workflow.
The script does not check for an existing association and is not intended for repeated creation.
{% else %}

Read-only: lists all association API records for the primary, selects the peer,
and displays the association details, including Active Data Guard status and apply lag.
No database password is needed. No unique match returns a nonzero exit code.
This cannot associate two independent databases, change an association, or query
configurations using the newer Data Guard Group API.
{% endif %}

[OCI CLI association commands](https://docs.oracle.com/en-us/iaas/tools/oci-cli/latest/oci_cli_docs/cmdref/db/data-guard-association.html).
